A||||78||stingant||3.4dohc||||1||
Z||000000||stingant||12-01-2001||03:55 PM||||I have a chance to get my hands on a 96 dohc 3.4 with 28000 miles. I would to put in in my 87 gt but how hard is it? I have read about shaving some of the cradle, but can someone give me complete details on what to do and what I'm getting myself into. And on a scale of 1-10 how hard do you think it was?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Spektrum-87GT||12-01-2001||04:40 PM||||i've heard the hardest part is the electrical wiring(piggybacking the wiring harness). I'm pretty sure it bolts right up to the getrag and isuzu... i could be wrong. there are a few sites out there that could help you... i'm not sure if anyone in here has done it, its a pretty rare swap compared to the other ones.||||reg||1
Z||000002||msweldon||12-01-2001||04:52 PM||||check out <BR> <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/swap/" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/swap/</A> <P>should be everything you need to know.  Do not go buy a conversion kit, its too simple of a swap. <P>Use your brain with a "little" fabrication and you'll have it done.<P>You'll need new cradle bushings, hoses, reroute the fuel lines, slight mod to the throttle cable, idler pully for PS pump, haynes manual for both cars to get the wiring diagrams and you'll need to fab a torque strut, and that's it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DKOV||12-01-2001||10:45 PM||||Then again, WITH A conversion kit, many of the disaster lurking steps are performed by a professional that had completed it a thousand time...  Worth the extra few bucks?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Also, they have laid out the procedure step by step so you don't find out, after it is installed, that "thing" you should have done and now have to pull it all out again!<P>For the DOHC (or TDC) swap, depending on the year of Fiero, there is often a small trim to the frame and some reinforcement.  Nothing major.<P>Keep in mind, when you do the swap, you will need MORE than just the motor...<P>You'll need the ECU as well... IMPORTANT!!!<P>If you use the Fiero tranny, most of the running gear should lide ride in but if you are using the Getrag 284 tranny, then you'll need the DOHC's axles, shifter linkage and other small items.<P>All this aside... DO IT!!!  This is the PERFECT motor for the Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The SOUND is that of Ferrari and the Power is to be reconned with!  It's WORTH the trouble.<P>Good Luck!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000004||kempo||12-01-2001||11:43 PM||||DKOV- check your pm!||||reg||1
Z||000005||LoW_KeY||12-02-2001||05:56 AM||||You know I wish I would've stuck one of these puppies in instead of my 94 camaro one.. oh well I had the 3.4 put in by my dads friend and I didn't want to make it more stressful, could've made it that stressful by using that 3.4 or going with a 3.8 SC  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Plan to build up my current 3.4 should be jump afterwards I hope.<P>-KC<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FierFormula88   <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kcbanner.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark Matthews||12-02-2001||09:49 AM||||So what car is a donner car for the 3.4 and the Getrag?  I'm interested in this, but don't know much about it.  Guess I should look at the site that MSWELDEN posted.  Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||12-02-2001||11:31 AM||||What trannie was used on the few rare 5 speed cars with the 3400dohc? I know GM didnt make allot of them, I think it was jsut early lumina Z34's. Not sure though.<P><BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000008||DKOV||12-02-2001||11:57 AM||||My Motor and tranny came out of a 1991 Grand Prix GTP.  It is the 284 Getrag although I have also seen it referenced at the 264 Getrag.  In either case, it was the 5-speed that came with the motor in that car.  <P>From what I have found, these 5-speed versions were VERY rare indeed.  I'm sure it is the same tranny from the Lumina Z34.<P>Several months ago, I checked with a local dealer to see if these motor/tranny combos were still available and they are.  At a premium though.  They are rare.<P>If the 5 speed is out, the Auto 4T60-E, is a very stout and quick tranny built expressly for use with these motors.  They are much more abundant but lack the higher revs and control of the 284 Getrag.  Option though.<P>In any case, you CAN mate the 3.4 DOHC/TDC to the Getrag 282.  No axle changes needed.  The gearing is not ideal for the new motor but still VERY punchy and fast.<P>Just remember to get <B>the</B> or a replacement ECU.  I can't state that enough.  These motors will NOT work with the Fiero ECU.  As such, you need to have the engine harness off the 3.4 combined with the harness from the Fiero.  It's not difficult, just time consuming.<P>Again, as if I haven't said this enough... It is WORTH IT!!!  LOVE my motor!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||12-02-2001||01:11 PM||||If it's a 96, it's definitely an automatic. No manuals after 93. Flash PCM too although it should run on the earlier 9396 ECM (which supports auto or manual). <P>That's some low miles!<P>For god's sake don't use a Getrag 284. There is no reason to. You can't even get parts for them, only complete rebuilds at around $1600-1800 dollars.<P>1-10?, It's a 1-10 depending on your skills.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||stingant||12-02-2001||01:32 PM||||Well I'm planning on using my Getrag with this motor. I heard that I would have to change the axles, but WHY? It doesn't come with the computer, but looks like all the wiring is there. There is no donor car. It was sitting in a junk yard showroom. I also heard that I would have to get the shift linkage of it, WHY would I that if im using my 5-spd. Will my flyweel bolt on?||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||12-02-2001||02:16 PM||||Hmmm, you will need to work out a throttle cable, the Fiero Getrag will work, you need a neutral balance flywheel, other than that it's not that bad of a swap. New AC lines. Exhaust system. What else folks?<P>The drivetrain from the Fiero/Getrag will work. Nothing needed there.<P>How much are they asking for the motor?<P>TK<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||DRH||12-02-2001||02:17 PM||||You won't have to change axles or shifter linkage if you're using the Fiero tranny. I think the confusion comes from people that use a different tranny.<P>The 88 Fiero 2.8 flywheel will work because it's neutrally balanced. 85-87 are balanced differently and <B>won't</B> work. I believe they will bolt up but will cause vibration. I'm not sure if it's practical to re-balance one or not. Probably best for you to just buy a 3.4 DOHC flywheel (or an 88 Fiero one if you run across one).||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||12-02-2001||02:24 PM||||I believe all of the 3.1L MPFI engines in the yards use a neutral flywheel so there shouldn't be a problem getting one. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||stingant||12-02-2001||03:13 PM||||Well guys Im getting that engine for only $600. Not bad AH. It only comes with the engine wiring, but im gunna see if i could get the wiring of another 96 dohc, with the computer also, and the computer has to be off a manual, right? If so, i won't find one, ill have to order it. I hear that it fits on an 88 cradle, will it fit with an 87...i know that there is some variation between the two (ie. E-brake cable,...)<p>[This message has been edited by stingant (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||FormulaJoe||12-02-2001||05:16 PM||||I am doing the same swap, but with a 3.4 TDC ('91 I believe, basically the same engine)  Mechanically it looks straight forward using the existing tranny and axles.  I have an '88 so I plan on using the flywheel from my 2.8 (neutral balance).  I am planning on using a front manifold on the back side (do not think you can do that with the later 3.4 DOHC) and making a "Y" pipe to connect to the stock Fiero exhaust.   Throttle cables, water and fuel all look do-able with a little fabrication and some thought.   Like you I bought just the motor (0 miles, how could I pass it up?) but it does have the engine wiring harness just not the one from the ECM to the engine.  I managed to pick up a 16149396 ECM, how do you tell if it is for a manual or an automatic?  Right now I am looking for the connectors (four-22 pin) or a harness that goes to the ECM.   Biggest hurdle seems to be the wiring harness, and that is just a matter of working out what goes where and putting it together, not difficult just time consuming. I was practicing my soldering today, think I will get a new iron before I start the harness!<P>------------------<BR>Yellow Formula<BR>(soon to be 3.4 DOHC)||||reg||6
Z||000016||87GTZ34||12-02-2001||09:11 PM||||Stingant,<P>  You won't find a std ecm for '96.  If you use the electronics from an earlier year, you can go standard.  The OBD II doesn't have production code for a standard trans.  Yo should also try to get the tansaxle for the TDC/DOHC as the gearing is better matched to that engine.||||reg||1
Z||000017||msweldon||12-03-2001||12:06 AM||||you can purchase and wire up a 93 stickshift ECM to a 96+ DOHC.  Only one or two wires do not align, no biggie, and you'll lose one of the two knock sensors the 96+ DOHC has.  If you have the money and time, get a programmable ecu such as a haltech or autronic.<P>FieroDave also can make a balanced aluminum flywheel for an '88 2.8 which should fit the DOHC.  Talk about a revver.<P>The 93 stick ecm doesn't run in SFI mode, only batch, but I believe even the 96 ecm switches to batch fire above 3 or 4K rpm. Terry you can probably correct me on this.<P><BR>msw<p>[This message has been edited by msweldon (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||12-03-2001||12:11 AM||||No that's right. SFI is only under 2500-3000 on most if not all of the GM engines.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Formula88||12-03-2001||12:54 AM||||Man, I wish I could find a 3.4DOHC for $600!!  I gotta start looking around here!  ||||reg||1
Z||000020||stingant||12-03-2001||12:14 PM||||what kind of prices I'm I gunna run into with a haltech computer or other personalized computers compared to OEM ones?||||reg||1
Z||000021||msweldon||12-03-2001||03:38 PM||||For a haltech your gonna need the E6K.  With the haltech your looking at about 800-1200, depending on your source but don't forget to include installation and dyno time.  Programmable ECU's are "not" for the weak at heart, but you can do damn near anything with them.  They can unlock the most potential out of any upgraded engine.<P>The "best" one out there is the Motec brand, semi-auto tuning, cockpit controller, and graphical tuning on a windows laptop where all others are DOS based.<P>If your on a budget I'd go with the 93' stick ecm and have terry burn you a prom with some customizations.  If your gonna turbo it, you might be able to go with a Gran Prix Turbo ECU, or a BUICK GN, due to the DOHC ECU's not having a 1+ Bar tables.<P>later||||reg||1
Z||000022||stingant||12-03-2001||05:16 PM||||Terry explain what burning a prom is? Could you modify an ecm for me? IF I go with a 93' stick ecm what modification will need to be done?||||reg||1
Z||000023||terryk||12-03-2001||08:25 PM||||Burning a prom (programable read only memory) means to load the memory chip with the program and calibration information for that ECM and engine. The 91-93 ECM's uses an eprom (eraseable), so you read the chip, make the changes to the calibrations, erase the eprom and reload it with the new settings. This called "burning" a prom or eprom. <P>It's called burning because early chips required that you literally burn out links in the chip. Obviously these can't be erased. Later they came up with proms that could be erased (with UV) and reused but the phrase "burn" stuck. <P>GM started out using OTP's (one-time-programable) proms. You can identify them because they don't have the clear window on the top. You can't erase them. Later, GM started using eproms. Eventually they went to eeproms. They can be erased without using UV.<P>Can I make one? Depends on what you want to change. Email me offline.<P>ps, you can run the GPT code int a DOHC's 9396. We did for some testing. Seemed to work fine. The wiring it almost identical too. At your own risk.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||stingant||12-03-2001||10:01 PM||||A friend of mine (89 Shelby Daytona) is running an automatic computer with his stick, why can't I just use any 3.4 dohc ecm?||||reg||1
Z||000025||Joe Torma||12-03-2001||10:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by msweldon:<BR><B>The "best" one out there is the Motec brand, semi-auto tuning, cockpit controller, and graphical tuning on a windows laptop where all others are DOS based.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Electromotive TEC-II/III are windows based.  I've got the TEC-II on the 3400.  Nice setup...lots of parameters, lots of money.<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||terryk||12-04-2001||12:06 AM||||I guess you can if you want. I don't recommend it because I don't want to deal with the annoying gremlins of auto code on a manual trans.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||lowCG||12-04-2001||12:08 PM||||I've got the auto ECM,works great,had to add some large resistors in lieu of the shifter solenoids,just used an a/f controller from SplitSecond to get the mixture right with the bigger exhaust.<BR>The throttle cable hooks up easily with a different end attached to thge cable;I made one out of a small piece of tubing.<BR>The stock flywheel was re-balanced/surfaced for $70;the Getrag284 flywheel will not work with the Fiero Getrags.<BR>That's a great price for that motor,I'd say your best bet for an ECM is to get an early model year one and have Terry re-work it,if he's not too busy.<BR>That motor for that price is a real deal.||||reg||1
Z||000028||DRH||12-04-2001||01:11 PM||||I just bought a 94 3.4 DOHC and this looks like as good a thread as any for a few initial questions. My car is a 5 speed Formula.<P>It came with no wiring (what is there has been cut). I plan to run a 91-93 ECM anyway so I'm thinking I need to get an ECM and  harness from a car with a 16149396. Since 5 speed 3.4s aren't exactly plentiful, what flexibility do I have with the wiring harness? What about the ECM, can I get one from an automatic car and just switch the eproms? It looks like the same ECM was used on 94-95 3.1, would one of these work with the right eprom? It sounds like a stock 5 speed eprom should work, what's the best source for one?<P>I'm looking at the fuel rail and it looks like with some modifications I can turn it around and avoid running the lines back across the motor again. Has anyone tried this?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000029||88-DOHC||12-04-2001||03:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>I just bought a 94 3.4 DOHC and this looks like as good a thread as any for a few initial questions. My car is a 5 speed Formula.<P>It came with no wiring (what is there has been cut). I plan to run a 91-93 ECM anyway so I'm thinking I need to get an ECM and  harness from a car with a 16149396. Since 5 speed 3.4s aren't exactly plentiful, what flexibility do I have with the wiring harness? What about the ECM, can I get one from an automatic car and just switch the eproms? It looks like the same ECM was used on 94-95 3.1, would one of these work with the right eprom? It sounds like a stock 5 speed eprom should work, what's the best source for one?<P>I'm looking at the fuel rail and it looks like with some modifications I can turn it around and avoid running the lines back across the motor again. Has anyone tried this?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, thats what we ended up doing on my car. The tabs that bolt the fuel rail down had to be cut on mine, since they didn't line up when turned around and otherwise interfered.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||msweldon||12-04-2001||03:04 PM||||FWIW, if anyone is worrying about the DOHC over stressing the 282 getrag, Engineered Performance in Atlanta rebuilt 1FST2M6's 2M6 282 getrag with stronger bearings and what not.  Regardless, it took consistent 300+ lb/ft torque launches from an ACT 3puck clutch with little or no wear signs when GTBastard eventually took it out.<P>email 1fst2m6 for details.<P>my .02||||reg||1
Z||000031||DRH||12-04-2001||03:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88-DOHC:<BR><B> Yes, thats what we ended up doing on my car. The tabs that bolt the fuel rail down had to be cut on mine, since they didn't line up when turned around and otherwise interfered.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mine looks like 2 bolts will even line up. I'll have to cut two brackets off. I'll probably try to reattach them so they line up. How is yours attached now?<P>Is your swap complete? Where are you located? <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||88-DOHC||12-04-2001||03:32 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B> Mine looks like 2 bolts will even line up. I'll have to cut two brackets off. I'll probably try to reattach them so they line up. How is yours attached now?<P>Is your swap complete? Where are you located? <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have a 96 DOHC, so its slightly different then previous years. Yes, the swap is completed. I purchased this car from my friend that actually did the swap (with some help from me). Check out the following URL which shows most of what has been done to the car over the course of its mods.<P> <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/swap/" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/swap/</A> <P>Located in Northen California.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88-DOHC (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||DRH||12-04-2001||03:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88-DOHC:<BR><B>  <BR> <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/swap/" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/swap/</A> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mine's a 94 so it is a little different. I've seen that site before... Nice car, nice swap.||||reg||1
Z||000034||88-DOHC||12-04-2001||06:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B> Mine's a 94 so it is a little different. I've seen that site before... Nice car, nice swap.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks, although I don't have the porsche boxter rims nor his original wilwood brakes (those went on his wifes car). I have the 13" brake package from Ryane/Held and custom 18" wheels.  Going to do some body mods to it, and have been toying with adding a roots supercharger to the engine to give me some better low end torque (and generally because it would be neat to do it).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000035||DRH||12-05-2001||11:01 AM||||I thought about a SC also. On paper it seems like it would really wake up this engine (not to mention the wow factor). Other than electronics and fabricating an intake plenum, it looks like there might be a problem with the height too. You're limited to how far down it can go by the fuel rail. A scoop would work in the middle, but I think the drive may run into the "channel" part of the decklid.<P>I found this link to someone that was doing a SC on a DOHC. The email address bounces back though...<BR> <A HREF="http://mae.wvu.edu/~mhawkins/super34.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://mae.wvu.edu/~mhawkins/super34.htm</A> <P>The way the rear end is sitting high in the pics, I don't think he actually had it installed at that time.<P>For me, it would be a phase 2 project (if at all), getting it running right n/a will be enough of a challenge.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000036||88-DOHC||12-05-2001||04:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>I thought about a SC also. On paper it seems like it would really wake up this engine (not to mention the wow factor). Other than electronics and fabricating an intake plenum, it looks like there might be a problem with the height too. You're limited to how far down it can go by the fuel rail. A scoop would work in the middle, but I think the drive may run into the "channel" part of the decklid.<P>I found this link to someone that was doing a SC on a DOHC. The email address bounces back though...<BR> <A HREF="http://mae.wvu.edu/~mhawkins/super34.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://mae.wvu.edu/~mhawkins/super34.htm</A> <P>The way the rear end is sitting high in the pics, I don't think he actually had it installed at that time.<P>For me, it would be a phase 2 project (if at all), getting it running right n/a will be enough of a challenge.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, I have seen that web site. You are correct about the "channel" part of the hood. Nothing that some cutting and fiber glass reinforcing won't cure.  The only other problem I see for me, is the necessity to be smog legal every other year, so I need to be able to remove it and put the idler pulley back on. It may turn out that the idler pulley bracket would be in the way and would need to be removed. This would make it so I wouldn't be able to put the idler back on once the SC is removed.  This is all just my observation from looking at the engine and imagining placement. I will probably get ahold of an Eton M62 and do some preliminary fitting.<P>From looking at that site again, it does just look like he has it sitting on the engine, although the intake looks like its a custom job. In anycase, at that height it would stick through the decklid (which may have been his intent). <P>For me, I have everything working, so I am just looking at how to extend it to make it better.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The hardest part of this swap is just the wiring harness. The rest is actually pretty straight forward and doable in a weekend or two.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||stingant0||12-06-2001||11:14 PM||||Why is the 96-97 dohc rated so badly by consumer reports? What goes wrong with them...I hear they are fragile engines.||||reg||1
Z||000038||lowCG||12-07-2001||12:23 AM||||Those Consumer reports are apt to change from year to year with the same comodity;they just say what they want to say when they say it.<BR>My mom got a VW Passat that was reported by Consumer Reports to be a great car,next year they changed their tune.The year after that she traded the car off with it's funky electronic tranny,and suspension with an inch of usable travel,yeah,great car.<BR>Would be good to find out more to see if it amounts to anything or not,I'm curious.||||reg||1
Z||000039||88-DOHC||12-07-2001||12:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stingant0:<BR><B>Why is the 96-97 dohc rated so badly by consumer reports? What goes wrong with them...I hear they are fragile engines.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Fragile?  Dunno about you, but any V6 engine that is able to and survive reving to 8000 rpm, isn't fragile. In my opinion these engines are no more fragile then any other  engine. Yes, some engines are simply lemons (like my first car, 84 Ford Escort), but I don't consider these one of them.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000040||stingant0||12-07-2001||12:57 AM||||I meant fragile because " The 3.4L takes frequent, extensive maintanence to assure its durability. All strange noises should be investigated immediately, as you may soon be walking. Maybe you arent mechanically inclined. That's ok, as long as youu can work with assistance. " that is an exert from <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html</A>  thats what I meant.||||reg||1
Z||000041||88-DOHC||12-07-2001||01:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stingant0:<BR><B>I meant fragile because " The 3.4L takes frequent, extensive maintanence to assure its durability. All strange noises should be investigated immediately, as you may soon be walking. Maybe you arent mechanically inclined. That's ok, as long as youu can work with assistance. " that is an exert from <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html</A>  thats what I meant.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, if you think about it, any strange noise from any engine should be investigated immediately.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I am not entirely sure why they would say that for the DOHC. It has no rods, no rockers, etc... I would say the only thing you really need to keep an eye on is the belts that drive the cams.  Everything else is pretty much the same no matter what engine you have. <P>There was a time when engines would bend the valves if a cam/timing belt broke (not all engines did that). I dunno if that applies to the DOHC, but that maybe why they stated it was fraggle. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000042||DKOV||12-07-2001||01:47 AM||||Here's what <B>I</B> know about the 3.4L DOHC (or TDC as I know it)...<P>I run the friggin crud out of it and it never complains, groans, howls or stumbles.  I'm terribly hard on teh clutch, constantly run it to redline and otherwise abuse it endlessly.<P>ONCE I spun a rod bearing, but that was because I, while racing a Mustang GT on the Freeway (I know - BAD me), I nearly missed my exit and downshifted from 5th to 3th at around 150...  OOps...  Some time in the shop and a new bottom end, and I'm back on the road.<P>I race this car at PIR and it is a daily run-about.  Spinning my tires is almost my most favorite thing in the world to do... Next to ruining a Ricer's day and eating up TAs, Camaros and Vettes.<P>Of course, now that I have my Lotus Esprit Turbo, my Little 3.4L Fiero gets serious competiton for stick time but, I'd NEVER do the things in my Lotus that I can do with the 3.4L.  The Lotus wouldn't take it!!!<P>Similar performance though... 0-60 in the mid 4s, 0 - 100 in 10...  Plus is SOUNDS so incredible!!!<P>It's a good thing I'm transplanting a Ferrari 355 body onto it this winter because it is already all Ferrari underneath   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>That sound... that SOUND!!!  It's the best music I can think of   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>All that to say...????<P>Don't believe someone's over inflated sense of forboading and selfworth (no offense).  Everyone take great pride in the warnings they offer.  However, ANY motor is subject to damage if neglected and ignored.  Just pay attention, service and fix what you need to and...<P>HAVE FUN!!!!<P>DKOV -<p>[This message has been edited by DKOV (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000043||stingant0||12-07-2001||02:01 AM||||DKOV - your engine is supercharged right? Can you send me some pics? Thanks for the info...I'm trying to convence my dad that doing a swap would be worth the time. Thanx<P>ps i want to see your Lotus...I love the Esprit...I'll take a dark grey Lotus any day over a viper.||||reg||1
Z||000044||88-DOHC||12-07-2001||02:04 AM||||AMEN!<P>Yes, mine has taken a beating as well, and it still keeps taking it.<P>BTW, you did mean 3.4 not 4.3?  A little dyslexia perhaps!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B>Here's what [b]I</B> know about the 4.3L DOHC (or TDC as I know it)...<P>I run the friggin crud out of it and it never complains, groans, howls or stumbles.  I'm terribly hard on teh clutch, constantly run it to redline and otherwise abuse it endlessly.<P>ONCE I spun a rod bearing, but that was because I, while racing a Mustang GT on the Freeway (I know - BAD me), I nearly missed my exit and downshifted from 5th to 3th at around 150...  OOps...  Some time in the shop and a new bottom end, and I'm back on the road.<P>I race this car at PIR and it is a daily run-about.  Spinning my tires is almost my most favorite thing in the world to do... Next to ruining a Ricer's day and eating up TAs, Camaros and Vettes.<P>Of course, now that I have my Lotus Esprit Turbo, my Little 3.4L Fiero gets serious competiton for stick time but, I'd NEVER do the things in my Lotus that I can do with the 3.4L.  The Lotus wouldn't take it!!!<P>Similar performance though... 0-60 in the mid 4s, 0 - 100 in 10...  Plus is SOUNDS so incredible!!!<P>It's a good thing I'm transplanting a Ferrari 355 body onto it this winter because it is already all Ferrari underneath   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>That sound... that SOUND!!!  It's the best music I can think of   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>All that to say...????<P>Don't believe someone's over inflated sense of forboading and selfworth (no offense).  Everyone take great pride in the warnings they offer.  However, ANY motor is subject to damage if neglected and ignored.  Just pay attention, service and fix what you need to and...<P>HAVE FUN!!!!<P>DKOV -[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000045||Formula88||12-07-2001||10:43 AM||||DKOV, I'd love to hear a recording of that SOUND!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hopefully, I'll catch you at some shows next year and get to check it out in person....||||reg||14
Z||000046||DKOV||12-07-2001||02:29 PM||||88-DOHC...<P>Yeah, got me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I fixed it though...  3.4 L<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000047||DKOV||12-07-2001||02:38 PM||||stingant0:<P>My 3.4L is NOT Supercharged although that sounds like fun!!!  I don't know if it needs any more power.  Truthfully, it's a bit scary as it is  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I do have a supercharger sitting on my owrkbench that I have been eyeing for the 3.4 for some time, but I just can't bring myself to do it.<P>I started from a Stock 215 HP 3.4L DTC and rebuilt it from the block up with all the goodies like port/polish on the heads and intake manifolds (yes there are two), some valve work, custom exhaust, underdrive pulley, fuel pressure riser, intake, etc.<P>Mathematically, the enging tops out at 322.8 HP although I have yet to dyno it for "at the wheel" HP.  All this while normally aspirated.<P>Adding a SC would boost the HP to 400+ and I'm not sure that the car, the tranny or the driver could handle that!!!<P>My Lotus, on the other hand, has 330 HP while some of the most recent models have 450 HP.  With the ECU tweak, the boost levels can be increased to some 500+ HP!!!<P>Why is this a BAD thing?  Even on the Lotus, that much HP is busting driveshafts when clutch-dumping!<P>I'm pretty happy with the 3.4 as it sits.  For now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>By the way, my Lotus is Black, just like my Fiero and my Durango R/T (also Supercharged and tuned to nearly 500 HP  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).<P>I can't help myself...  I raced my first official race two years ago and I've been addicted to speed and power ever since  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000048||DKOV||12-07-2001||02:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>DKOV, I'd love to hear a recording of that SOUND!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hopefully, I'll catch you at some shows next year and get to check it out in person....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I should do that!  I never thought of that... but, how do I upload it for you to hear?  I've never done anything like that on a PC before.  Also, the car won't be ready for any shows until the 355 swap is done.  HOPEFULLY it will be ready for the paint by this summer as I havn't really started tearing it up yet...  It's to much fun while it runs, why would I tear it down so I can't drive it???<P>To drive it WITHOUT a body!!! THAT'S why!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyway, keep in touch and I'll keep you posted.<P>DKOV -<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000049||Formula88||12-07-2001||03:04 PM||||Well, getting the recording, if you have a laptop with a microphone, or can run a mic to your PC, just record it with soundrec.exe and record it straight to a .wav file.  If you don't have somewhere to post the file for download, I'm sure someone on here could help.  <P>Of course, you could email it directly to me, if you wanted!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000050||stingant0||12-07-2001||05:49 PM||||So porting and polishing the heads and intake do alot for the 3.4. I connected my dad's GM tech (he was on the 2.5 team with the fiero) and gunna see if he can look for the original cams. THAT WOULD SO AWSOME. He was really busy this summer working on computer stuff, but those 89 prototyes still exist--but he couldn't find them. Steve Oakley is his name.||||reg||1
Z||000051||stingant0||12-08-2001||12:28 PM||||Can so of you 3.4 owners put some pics of your exhaust? I'm contemplating on how to run my exhaust.||||reg||1
Z||000052||lowCG||12-08-2001||01:31 PM||||I would run the exhaust in the stock manner,which is going to be a bit more of a hassle than the way I did it,which is simple and loud.<BR>The 3.4 exhaust dumps in the rear,almost perfectly centered,using this,I ran a pipe down to "Y",and out into some hi-tech stainless glasspack-type mufflers,then out to some resonated tips in the stock locations.<BR>I might put some cats on it(VW GTI)in the small space above the mufflers,parallel to the mufflers,then do a 180 back to the mufflers.||||reg||1
Z||000053||88-DOHC||12-08-2001||03:20 PM||||Well, in smog legal mode, my exhaust is very simple. It uses the stock manifolds and Y pipe (which as been said, its exit point is at the rear). Attached to that is a cat. Attached to the cat is a small 45 degree elbow. Attached to that is a single custom made supertrapp that uses the stock exhaust mounting bracket.  The whole exhaust is probably around 3 feet long. Its loud and sometimes obnoxious, but it sure does sound good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>In smog illegal mode, its dual custom headers into "spin-tech" like mufflers, into the custom supertrapp mufflers, one each side. No Y pipe, and is true dual exhaust. In this mode its even more obnoxious then smog legal mode when winding it up, but I think is quieter when cruising.||||reg||1
Z||000054||DKOV||12-08-2001||08:16 PM||||My set uses the 3.4L Y pipe and that points straight out as stated above.  From there, it turns 90 degrees right and runs about 6 inches before making a 180 down and back to the left.  The CAT is inserted here, centered under the Y pipe exit.  After the CAT it makes another 180 back toward the passenger side and splits downward centered under the CAT.  Each pipe runs toward the wheels with a BULLET pack inserted there.  After the Bullet, the pipes make a 90 degree out to the stock exhaust exits.  They are capped with twin Monzas on each side (total of four pipes show) similar to the stock looking GT exhaust without the trumpet flares.<P>If that is comfusing, I'll try and get a pic for "visual aid"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I hope that helps!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000055||DRH||12-08-2001||08:46 PM||||The plan for mine is to get another front manifold, flip it over and install it on the back. Then I'll need to get a custom crossover made similar to the stock Fiero one. The rest of the exhaust will then follow the stock Fiero path. Phase one may actually use the stock Fiero exhaust.<P>This will work on the 91-95 engines, I don't know if the manifold will flip on the 96-97 engines.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000056||88-DOHC||12-09-2001||02:15 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DRH:<BR><B>The plan for mine is to get another front manifold, flip it over and install it on the back. Then I'll need to get a custom crossover made similar to the stock Fiero one. The rest of the exhaust will then follow the stock Fiero path. Phase one may actually use the stock Fiero exhaust.<P>This will work on the 91-95 engines, I don't know if the manifold will flip on the 96-97 engines.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Not sure, will have to look on mine and see if it will.  The only problem you might face is if your state has smog laws, you might have to plumb all the necassary sensors beyond the O2 sensor that you will put in your Y pipe.  Of course the disadvantage of doing it Fiero style is that you won't really be able to have true dual exhaust (not that it probably matters all that much).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88-DOHC (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000057||stingant0||12-09-2001||03:51 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3.4_1.JPG"> <P>here is the engine guys. How loud would this car be with only four glass packs. ||||reg||1
Z||000058||stingant0||12-11-2001||03:22 PM||||How about those exhaust pics!!||||reg||1
Z||000059||DKOV||12-11-2001||04:36 PM||||Four Glass Packs...  As loud as Mine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>What is up with that engine?  Is it the film or was that thing in a Car Fire?  Looks awfully grey.<P>About sound...  Sound and loudness is a very easy way to justify adding a Turbo!  The turbo itself acts like a big 'ol muffler.  Handy then, in a car with no SPACE for a muffler, wouldn't you say  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I have a CAT, two bullet packs and two monzas.  My car is loud enough where the V8 guys ask me to keep it down!<P>This is a pic that I had found to provide the inspiration for my exhaust.  My setup is a bit different in that I have an additional switch back and the addition of the two bullets and monzas.  You get the basic idea though...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.americancustomeng.com/images/Mvc-154x.jpg"> <P>I hope that helps!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000060||88-DOHC||12-11-2001||05:28 PM||||I will see if I can get the P.O.S. digital camera that I got for free working and snap a couple pictures.  But mine is so simple its ridiculous. The 96 DOHC exhaust manifold exits out the rear at an angle toward the passenger side of the car not straight down like in the picture of the SC 3800 above (at least I think thats a 3800SC). Anyway, because of that it was easier just to build a single exhaust.<p>[This message has been edited by 88-DOHC (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000061||stingant0||12-11-2001||06:16 PM||||Well the camera I have my dad got for $19. He didn't like it so he bought a nice $400 one. Well I took the cheap one the college. The enigne has been sitting for about 2 years, and it is dirty. I just bought it today.||||reg||1
Z||000062||DKOV||12-11-2001||07:24 PM||||Yeah, that pic is a 3800SC motor, but my year of 3.4L TDC has vitually the same position and angle of exit.  That's why I gave it to my exhaust guy for an example.<P>I wanted... no, NEEDED a dual exhaust, so this made the most sense.<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000063||88-DOHC||12-11-2001||07:36 PM||||With headers I have a dual exhaust, but then I don't have a cat thus its illegal here in California. Which means every other year, I would have to remove the headers and reinstall all the smog crap again. Its all a pain in the butt.  When I get motivated to do something about it and figure out a way to make it quieter, perhaps I will re-address the current setup.  What I really need is an intergrated cat/muffler.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Man, I should move to a state that doesn't have smog laws, then I could just do whatever I wanted to the car and be done with it.||||reg||1
Z||000064||87GTZ34||12-11-2001||08:17 PM||||Here's a picture of the '96 manifolds and xover pipe.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DOHCexhausta.jpg">  <P>I am planning to get another front manifold and make an adapter then fabricate the y-pipe.  <P>I wanted to modify the rear manifold by cutting off the exit and capping it with welded iron but I have a fabrication shop (working on my mods) that has metalurgist on staff and he said it wouldn't hold up to the heat no less the added heat of turbo pressure.<P>He actually wanted me to go to his casting guy to have rear manifold recast to my specs but I would need sell quite a few to break even and didn't think their is a market for it.  I mean headers could be fabricated for double the money of the manifold and should flow better.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000065||88-DOHC||12-11-2001||08:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Here's a picture of the '96 manifolds and xover pipe.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DOHCexhausta.jpg">   <P>I am planning to get another front manifold and make an adapter then fabricate the y-pipe.  <P>I wanted to modify the rear manifold by cutting off the exit and capping it with welded iron but I have a fabrication shop (working on my mods) that has metalurgist on staff and he said it wouldn't hold up to the heat no less the added heat of turbo pressure.<P>He actually wanted me to go to his casting guy to have rear manifold recast to my specs but I would need sell quite a few to break even and didn't think their is a market for it.  I mean headers could be fabricated for double the money of the manifold and should flow better.<P>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 12-11-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yep thats the manifold setup on my 96. For people that don't have to worry about smog crap, you can flip over a front manifold and use it on the back (I think) and custom make a Y or whatever. For places like California where you have to get all sorts of crap done to make your transplat legal its far easier just to stick with the stock setup. I have headers for mine, but every other year when I have to get it smogged I would have to remove them and put everything back into smog legal mode. So at the moment, the headers are not on the car. ||||reg||1
Z||000066||87GTZ34||12-11-2001||08:36 PM||||88, On the '96 and up the manifold won't flip as is because they changed the ports and exits.  You can however make an adapter and flip it.||||reg||1
Z||000067||88-DOHC||12-12-2001||02:17 AM||||Ok, wasn't sure as my manifolds are in the car, therefor I can't really look at them to see if they could be flipped over and used. From looking at the above pic again though, I see the mounting holes are different anyway. Seems like if your going to make an adapter you might as well just make a custom manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000068||lowCG||12-12-2001||08:49 PM||||Headers with 2" primaries(drool,slurp)would be very cool,if one had the time.||||reg||1
Z||000069||artherd||12-13-2001||12:16 AM||||Too big, 1.5" (or een 1.3") would be about right, in 24" legenth, for a 7,000rpm peak.<P>The 3.4TDC does not have the same individual cylinder cc as a 502cid V8 :P{<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>Headers with 2" primaries(drool,slurp)would be very cool,if one had the time.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000070||stingant0||12-14-2001||11:47 PM||||There is the guy at my school with a 96 T/A with a 6-spd. He is really cocky and tells me that his car can beet any v6, he also says he has 290HP we all know he has 275HP. His car is completely stock. HELP ME. With the engine out of the car, what mods can I do to beat him. I want to help rename the Fiero from "Oh is your car is so cute" to "What a beast". How much HP can I get out of this thing with some inexpensive mods? I know speed costs money but I hear this engines are limited and the power can be unleashed easily. With help from Terry can a burned computer make it faster, with an underdrive pulley kit can I increase the red line?( with a 2.8 pulley kit work on this engine with a new belt that is). Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000071||artherd||12-15-2001||12:46 AM||||the 3.4 TDC/DOHC can make up to 290-300bhp with some extensive mods (talk to DKOV, he's the 3.4 moding expert  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>If you want to go DOHC forced induction... Talk to West Coast Fiero/www.cwestco.com. I've seen their car in person, looks fast  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>But even tossing in a stock TDC if all you want your car to do is beat a heavy all iron LT1 firechicken you'll be set.<P>PS: skip moding GM computers, go full afrermarket Electromotive TEC-II or TEC-III. Anything less is a waste of your time.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
